+# Platform Provider API
+
+A platform provider is a process that holds passkeys for the local machine,
+for example an authenticator backed by a TPM or a secure element.
+credentialsd offers each provider to the user as a device with the
+`internal` transport and forwards create and get requests to it.
+
+credentialsd builds `clientDataJSON` and checks the origin and RP ID as it
+does for every other transport. The provider receives the hash of
+`clientDataJSON`, the RP ID and the origin, and returns authenticator data
+and signatures. It never sees the request JSON.
+
+## Registration
+
+A provider registers with a drop-in file named `<id>.conf`. credentialsd
+reads these directories, highest priority first:
+
+1. `/etc/credentialsd/providers`
+2. `/usr/local/share/credentialsd/providers`
+3. `/usr/share/credentialsd/providers`
+
+The first file for an ID wins, so a file in `/etc` overrides a packaged
+one, and an empty file disables it. Debug builds also read the
+colon-separated directories in `CREDSD_PROVIDER_DIRS` first. The file stem
+must only contain ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `-` and `_`.
+
+```ini
+[Provider]
+# Shown in logs. Required.
+Name=Example Platform Authenticator
+# Well-known name on the session bus. Required.
+BusName=org.example.Passkeys1
+# Object that implements the interface. Required.
+ObjectPath=/org/example/Passkeys1
+# Defaults to xyz.iinuwa.credentialsd.Provider1.
+Interface=org.example.Passkeys.Provider1
+# Optional. When set, credentialsd only sends requests to the owner of
+# BusName if that process runs this executable.
+Executable=/usr/libexec/example-passkeyd
+# Optional. The only supported value is "internal".
+Transport=internal
+```
+
+The provider should also ship a D-Bus service file for `BusName`, so that it
+is started on demand.
+
+credentialsd calls providers from the connection that owns
+`xyz.iinuwa.credentialsd.Credentials`. A provider should answer only that
+connection: check that the sender of each call is the current owner of that
+name and that it runs as the same user.
+
+## Interface
+
+```
+GetInfo() -> a{sv}
+ListCredentials(s rp_id, aay allow) -> aa{sv}
+MakeCredential(u request_id, ay client_data_hash, a{sv} rp, a{sv} user,
+               ai algorithms, aay exclude, a{sv} options) -> a{sv}
+GetAssertion(u request_id, ay client_data_hash, s rp_id, ay credential_id,
+             a{sv} options) -> a{sv}
+Cancel(u request_id)
+signal UserActionNeeded(u request_id, s kind, a{sv} details)
+```
+
+Integer values in dictionaries may use any D-Bus integer type.
+
+### GetInfo() -> a{sv}
+
+Returns what the provider supports. This must be cheap and must not prompt
+the user. credentialsd calls it to build the device list, before each
+request and for `GetClientCapabilities()`. A provider that does not answer
+within two seconds is left out.
+
+- `name` `s`: display name.
+- `aaguid` `ay`: the provider's AAGUID.
+- `algorithms` `ax`: supported COSE algorithm identifiers.
+- `uv` `as`: user verification methods, for example `biometric` or
+  `password`. An empty list means the provider cannot verify the user.
+- `extensions` `as`: supported WebAuthn extension identifiers.
+- `locked` `b`: the provider cannot be used until the user unlocks it, for
+  example by logging in. The UI is told with `NotifyInternalLocked`.
+
+### ListCredentials(rp_id: s, allow: aay) -> aa{sv}
+
+Lists credentials for `rp_id`. With a non-empty `allow` list, only
+credentials in the list are returned. This must not prompt the user or use
+a credential's key. Each entry has:
+
+- `id` `ay`: credential ID. Required.
+- `user_handle` `ay`
+- `user_name` `s`
+- `display_name` `s`
+
+When a provider returns no credentials, it takes no further part in the
+request. When it returns more than one, the user picks one.
+
+### MakeCredential(...) -> a{sv}
+
+- `request_id` `u`: chosen by credentialsd, used by `UserActionNeeded` and
+  `Cancel()`.
+- `client_data_hash` `ay`: SHA-256 of `clientDataJSON`.
+- `rp` `a{sv}`: `id` `s`, `name` `s`.
+- `user` `a{sv}`: `id` `ay`, `name` `s`, `displayName` `s`. These use the
+  member names of WebAuthn's `PublicKeyCredentialUserEntity`.
+- `algorithms` `ai`: COSE algorithm identifiers in order of preference.
+- `exclude` `aay`: credential IDs that must not be registered again.
+- `options` `a{sv}`:
+  - `origin` `s`: the request origin.
+  - `top_origin` `s`: the top-level origin, for cross-origin requests.
+  - `rk` `b`: whether a discoverable credential is requested.
+  - `uv` `s`: `required`, `preferred` or `discouraged`.
+  - `password_fd` `h`: see [Passwords](#passwords).
+
+Returns:
+
+- `attestation_object` `ay`: the CBOR attestation object with `fmt`,
+  `authData` and `attStmt`. Required.
+- `transports` `as`: defaults to `["internal"]`.
+
+### GetAssertion(...) -> a{sv}
+
+- `request_id` `u`, `client_data_hash` `ay`: as for `MakeCredential()`.
+- `rp_id` `s`
+- `credential_id` `ay`: a credential returned by `ListCredentials()`.
+- `options` `a{sv}`: `origin`, `top_origin`, `uv` and `password_fd`, as for
+  `MakeCredential()`.
+
+Returns:
+
+- `authenticator_data` `ay`. Required.
+- `signature` `ay`. Required.
+- `user_handle` `ay`: defaults to the user handle from `ListCredentials()`.
+
+credentialsd rejects authenticator data whose RP ID hash does not match the
+request.
+
+### Cancel(request_id: u)
+
+Cancels the call with the given request ID. credentialsd calls it when the
+user cancels or when another device completes the ceremony.
+
+### UserActionNeeded(request_id: u, kind: s, details: a{sv})
+
+Tells the UI what the provider is waiting for. `kind` is one of:
+
+- `touch`: on-device user verification, for example a fingerprint. Sent to
+  the UI as `NeedsUserVerification`. `details` may carry `retries` with the
+  number of attempts left.
+- `presence`: a user presence gesture. Sent to the UI as
+  `NeedsUserPresence`.
+
+Other kinds are ignored.
+
+### Passwords
+
+A provider that needs the user's password answers `MakeCredential()` or
+`GetAssertion()` with a `PasswordNeeded` error. credentialsd asks the UI for
+the password with `NotifyNeedsPassword` and calls the method again with a
+new `request_id` and the `password_fd` option: a file descriptor to read the
+password from until end of file. A provider that rejects the password
+answers `WrongPassword`, and the UI asks again.
+
+### Errors
+
+The last component of the D-Bus error name selects the meaning, so
+`org.example.Passkeys.Error.WrongPassword` works as well as any other
+prefix.
+
+- `PasswordNeeded`: call again with `password_fd`.
+- `WrongPassword`: the password was rejected. Call again with a new one.
+- `Locked`: the provider is locked.
+- `InvalidStateError`: a credential in `exclude` exists. Ends the ceremony.
+- `NotAllowedError`, `AbortError`: the user declined or the call was
+  cancelled. The provider takes no further part in the request; other
+  devices stay available.
+- `NotSupportedError`, `SecurityError` and anything else: the provider
+  takes no further part in the request.
+
